theory.intro:
Rotating a circular arrangement does not create a new one, because everyone keeps the same neighbours. Fixing one object's position removes the $n$ rotations, leaving $(n-1)!$ arrangements of $n$ distinct objects. If the circle can also be flipped over, divide by $2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\fill (90:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (30:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-30:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-90:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-150:2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (150:2) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (90:2.75) {$A$ (fixed)};
\node at (30:2.7) {$1$};
\node at (-30:2.7) {$2$};
\node at (-90:2.7) {$3$};
\node at (-150:2.7) {$\cdots$};
\node at (150:2.85) {$n-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
A keyring, bracelet or necklace can be flipped over, so a reflection is the same arrangement and the count is $\dfrac{(n-1)!}{2}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) circle (1.4);
\fill (90:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (0:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-90:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (180:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (90:1.85) {$A$};
\node at (0:1.85) {$B$};
\node at (-90:1.85) {$C$};
\node at (180:1.85) {$D$};
\draw[dashed] (2.5,-2.1) -- (2.5,2.1);
\node[above] at (2.5,2.1) {flip over};
\begin{scope}[shift={(5,0)}]
\draw (0,0) circle (1.4);
\fill (90:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (0:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (-90:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (180:1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (90:1.85) {$A$};
\node at (0:1.85) {$D$};
\node at (-90:1.85) {$C$};
\node at (180:1.85) {$B$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
